---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ResearchDiagram from '../components/ResearchDiagram.astro';
const questions = [
  { number: '01', title: 'How often do fixes change both code and assets?', text: 'Study bug-fixing histories in open-source Unity games to measure when source code and assets change together. Scenes, prefabs, materials, animation, and configuration are part of the system being studied.' },
  { number: '02', title: 'What kinds of defects need coordinated fixes?', text: 'Develop a taxonomy of code and asset co-fixing patterns. The aim is to describe the relationships behind the changes, and explain why a fix needed changes to more than one kind of file.' },
  { number: '03', title: 'Can those relationships help locate a bug?', text: 'Design and evaluate GameLoc, a proposed structure-aware approach to bug localization. Compare its ability to retrieve relevant code and assets against information retrieval baselines.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Research" description="Proposed MSc research at the University of Calgary: code and asset co-fixing in open-source Unity games, defect taxonomy, and structure-aware bug localization.">
  <header class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Research / Proposed MSc work</p><h1 class="display">Understanding bugs<br />across code and assets.</h1><p class="intro-copy">I'm an incoming MSc student in Computer Science at the University of Calgary. I plan to study bugs that involve both source code and game assets, such as scenes and prefabs.</p></header>
  <section class="section-grid" aria-labelledby="proposal-heading"><p class="overline">01 / The research question</p><div><p class="proposal-label">Empirical Analysis of Code and Asset Co-Fixing in Open-Source Video Games</p><h2 class="display section-title" id="proposal-heading">What changes when the fix spans code and assets?</h2><div class="body-copy"><p>In Unity, behavior depends on source code and on scenes, prefabs, materials, animation, and configuration. A bug can sit in the relationship between them. Looking only at code can miss part of the explanation.</p><p>My proposed research with <strong>Dr. Nimmi Weeraddana</strong> starts with repository histories from OSSGameBench. The plan is to examine coordinated code and asset changes, build a taxonomy of the defects behind them, and use that evidence to inform bug localization.</p></div><div class="proposal-figure"><ResearchDiagram /></div><ol class="questions">{questions.map(q => <li><span aria-hidden="true">{q.number}</span><div><h3 class="display">{q.title}</h3><p>{q.text}</p></div></li>)}</ol></div></section>
  <section class="section-grid" aria-labelledby="gameloc-heading"><p class="overline">02 / Proposed approach</p><div><h2 class="display section-title" id="gameloc-heading">GameLoc: a proposed<br />bug localization tool</h2><div class="body-copy"><p>The proposed approach combines lexical retrieval, semantic embeddings, and graph relationships between game artifacts. Unity GUID references offer one way to connect assets with the code and other assets they depend on.</p><p>The planned evaluation will compare this approach against information retrieval baselines. The question is whether adding structure helps identify the files relevant to a bug, particularly when the repair crosses from code into assets.</p></div><aside class="research-note"><p class="overline">Status: proposed</p><p>This is proposed graduate research. The taxonomy, GameLoc approach, and comparative evaluation are planned work. There are no published findings to report yet.</p></aside></div></section>
  <section class="section-grid" aria-labelledby="early-work-heading"><p class="overline">03 / Earlier exploration</p><div><h2 class="display section-title" id="early-work-heading">Earlier diagnostic<br />prototypes.</h2><div class="experiment"><div class="experiment-meta"><span>Python</span><span>Synthetic data</span></div><h3 class="display">CI build duration analysis</h3><p>A prototype that parses build logs into phases and explores relationships between phase duration, asset changes, and timeouts. Real build telemetry would test its assumptions against runner contention and cache variability.</p></div><div class="experiment"><div class="experiment-meta"><span>.NET 8 / C#</span><span>Synthetic data</span></div><h3 class="display">Game engine crash triage</h3><p>A prototype for normalizing stack traces, grouping repeated crashes, and mapping frames to possible source changes. Real crash reports would test the assumptions against inlining, interleaved threads, and less tidy failures.</p></div><aside class="research-note"><p class="overline">Scope of the evidence</p><p>Both prototypes were evaluated on synthetic fixtures. They demonstrate processing pipelines and exploratory heuristics. They are not published research or evidence of performance on production systems.</p></aside><a class="text-link" href="/prototypes">Read the prototype walkthrough</a></div></section>
  <section class="section-grid" aria-labelledby="research-context-heading"><p class="overline">04 / Practical grounding</p><div><h2 class="display section-title" id="research-context-heading">Relevant experience</h2><div class="body-copy"><p>At Ubisoft Halifax, I worked on gameplay systems in Unity and C#. At Teams Squared, I build internal software and lead IT operations and cybersecurity. My personal work includes <a href="/projects/openquire">OpenQuire</a>, an open-source PDF app, and <a href="/projects/ruster">Ruster</a>, an early Rust engine project with a shared 3D renderer and scene editor.</p><p>Working on these systems is what led me to this research topic. I want to investigate some of the debugging problems I encountered while developing games and software.</p></div><div class="research-links"><a class="text-link" href="/projects">Explore the projects</a><a class="text-link" href="mailto:mail@akilfernando.dev">Email me about research</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .proposal-figure { margin-top: 2rem; max-width: 550px; }
  .proposal-label { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .675rem; color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; max-width: 60ch; }
  .questions { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .questions li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; padding-block: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .questions li > span { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .7rem; color: var(--accent); }
  h3 { font-size: 1.75rem; line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: -.035em; }
  .questions p, .experiment > p { margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--ink-muted); max-width: 60ch; font-size: .9375rem; }
  .experiment { margin-top: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1.5rem; }
  .experiment-meta { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .675rem; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: .75rem; }
  .research-note { margin-top: 2.5rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  .research-note > p:last-child { margin-top: .65rem; font-size: .8125rem; color: var(--ink-muted); max-width: 65ch; }
  .research-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0 2rem; }
</style>
